xnu 0405: the framebuffer says it waits, so the client stops waiting twice

0350 made present() wait for the compositor's fence before it copies a
surface into the panel, but IOMobileFramebuffer never said so. The GL
interposer asks (display_waits(), tools/gpu/eagl.c); with no
MondrianWaitsForGPU property it keeps its own glFinish at the end of every
frame, which puts the CPU 2.4-6.2 ms behind the GPU for a wait the display
does anyway. Measured with the property absent: 'mondrian-gl: frame' shows
that wait under 'waiting', 2.4-4.4 ms on a page swipe and 6.2 ms on a
launch. The interposer then flushes with a fence and does not wait
(glClientWaitSync with a zero timeout), and the display's copy still
cannot read a surface the GPU is drawing.

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/arm/MondrianFramebuffer.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/arm/MondrianFramebuffer.cpp
--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/MondrianFramebuffer.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/MondrianFramebuffer.cpp
@@ -1031,6 +1031,15 @@
 	setProperty("external", false);
 	setProperty("DisplayPipePlaneBaseAlignment", 64ULL, 32);
 	/*
+	 * The GL interposer asks this before it decides to glFinish a frame
+	 * (`display_waits()` in tools/gpu/eagl.c). With the fence on, the copy in
+	 * present() is the wait: a client-side glFinish puts the CPU a whole GPU
+	 * time behind every frame and buys nothing the display does not do itself.
+	 */
+	if (mondrian_fb_gpuwait) {
+		setProperty("MondrianWaitsForGPU", kOSBooleanTrue);
+	}
+	/*
 	 * IOMobileFramebufferOpen's getEDTProperty() reads these as 4-byte
 	 * CFData (getBytes:range:). An OSNumber crashes on -length and an
 	 * OSString on -getBytes:range:, so publish raw u32 bytes.
